  "brief_md": "# A07 — Self-serve tax filing for US expat investors in Europe\n\n## Payer\nThe individual US expat files and pays for their own return (self-serve model, not B2B). Price hypothesis: $150-$200 per federal return for the investor tier, anchored to MyExpatTaxes' $175 all-in plan (https://www.myexpattaxes.com/) and Expatfile's $199 Investor tier.\n\n## Competitors\n- **Expatfile** — Standard $119 / Premier $159 / Investor $199 (capital gains, crypto, FATCA); 165k+ users, $100M+ refunds. https://expatfile.tax/\n- **MyExpatTaxes** — $175 flat, includes Federal + FBAR + Schedule C + Schedule D; 100k returns prepared. https://www.myexpattaxes.com/\n- **H&R Block Expat DIY** — $119 (employee) / $169 (investor) + $99/state + $49/FBAR + $99 per PFIC Form 8621. https://www.hrblock.com/expat-tax-preparation/diy-expat-tax-filing-online/\n- **ExpatTaxOnline (ETO)** — $74 flat but *explicitly excludes* dividends, capital gains, crypto, and income >$100k — i.e., it excludes exactly the investor segment this idea targets. https://www.expattaxonline.com/us-tax-self-preparation-software/\n- Atamatax, Top Tax — pricing not found in this pass.\n\nThis is a mature, six-competitor category, not a greenfield space.\n\n## Acquisition channel\n- **Paid search**: Finance/Insurance vertical average CPC ≈ $4.66 (WordStream 2024 benchmark), likely higher for a low-volume niche term like 'expat tax software'. https://www.wordstream.com/blog/2024-google-ads-benchmarks\n- **Referral programs**: Expatfile pays $120 cash per 3 referred paying customers (~$40 CAC). https://expatfile.tax/referral-program/\n- Expat Facebook groups and forums (AARO, ACA, r/USExpatTaxes) are a plausible low-cost/organic channel but no direct cost data was found; incumbents already have brand presence there.\n\n## Demand evidence\n- \"The PFIC rule is driving a lot of people crazy!!\" — https://www.expatforum.com/threads/pfic.1508122/\n- \"I gather I'll be on the hook for north of $50K... the filing expenses ... are going to be costly.\" — https://britishexpats.com/forum/usa-57/attorney-advising-pfics-936911/\n- \"It generally seems that expats don't even realise that they should file tax returns... I've seriously considered renouncing.\" — https://www.expatforum.com/threads/questions-about-pfic-and-fatca.1550590/\n\n## Case for\nPain is real, well-documented, and financially severe (five-figure remediation costs, renunciation consideration). Incumbents already prove people pay $120-$200/year for DIY software at scale (165k and 100k users respectively), and all of them upsell specifically for the investor/PFIC use case — signaling a validated, underserved sub-segment that a focused, AI-automated PFIC/Schedule-D tool could target with lower cost structure and lean channels (referrals, communities) rather than expensive paid search.

## Case against
Six well-capitalized, IRS-authorized incumbents already directly target this exact customer with mature, reviewed products. Becoming an Authorized IRS e-file Provider requires an application, suitability check, and fingerprinting that can take up to 45 days (IRS Pub 3112) — a serious obstacle to a six-week build-and-launch plan. The domain is high-stakes: PFIC/FBAR/FATCA errors can cost customers tens of thousands of dollars, demanding a trust level a brand-new one-person product will struggle to establish against competitors with thousands of reviews. The addressable niche (DIY-preferring, investment-holding, Europe-based US expats dissatisfied with existing $120-200 tools) is narrow, and paid acquisition in finance is comparatively costly.

## Open questions
See `open_questions` field — chiefly, whether EFIN approval timing is compatible with a six-week build, and whether a narrower PFIC-calculation tool (rather than a full competing filer) is the better wedge.
